{"id":"ai-prompt-techniques","title":"AI: prompt techniques that work","subject":"AI prompting","label":"AI prompting","exams":[],"language":"en","seconds":172,"duration":171.62,"audio":"voice.mp3","words":485,"sections":[{"id":"hook","title":"Start","start":0,"end":13.785},{"id":"explain","title":"Core techniques","start":13.785,"end":43.295},{"id":"explain2","title":"More techniques","start":43.295,"end":79.925},{"id":"example","title":"Worked example","start":79.925,"end":102.265},{"id":"check","title":"Quick check","start":102.265,"end":112.365},{"id":"answer","title":"Answer","start":112.365,"end":124.765},{"id":"mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","start":124.765,"end":147.435},{"id":"recap","title":"Recap","start":147.435,"end":171.62}],"lines":[{"s":0,"a":0.5,"b":4.89,"text":"Have you ever asked an AI for help and got a vague answer that missed the point?","w":[0.54,0.68,0.86,1.14,1.38,1.54,1.9,2.06,2.82,2.94,3.14,3.24,3.66,4,4.18,4.4,4.52]},{"s":0,"a":5.34,"b":8.94,"text":"It is usually not the AI alone. The way you ask matters.","w":[5.42,5.54,5.72,6.1,6.32,6.58,6.98,7.78,7.92,8.08,8.3,8.52]},{"s":0,"a":9.24,"b":13.36,"text":"I'm Kora, and in this lesson you will learn four prompt techniques that work.","w":[9.36,9.56,10.34,10.48,10.6,10.82,11.2,11.32,11.48,11.8,12.1,12.46,12.86,13.04]},{"s":1,"a":14.21,"b":19.56,"text":"First, give examples. Show the model one to three examples of the input and the output you want.","w":[14.24,14.6,14.8,15.92,16.14,16.24,16.68,16.84,16.94,17.18,17.8,17.9,18.08,18.5,18.6,18.74,19.08,19.22]},{"s":1,"a":19.86,"b":24.12,"text":"That is few shot prompting. Asking with no examples is zero shot.","w":[19.86,20.1,20.3,20.54,20.78,21.76,22.12,22.36,22.56,23.24,23.44,23.84]},{"s":1,"a":24.42,"b":28.32,"text":"The model copies the pattern, so make your examples good and varied.","w":[24.44,24.6,24.96,25.34,25.46,26.28,26.48,26.68,26.84,27.36,27.78,27.94]},{"s":1,"a":28.62,"b":34.99,"text":"Second, ask for step by step reasoning on problems with several steps, such as maths and logic.","w":[28.76,29.7,29.98,30.22,30.52,30.72,31.06,31.56,31.7,32.16,32.36,32.74,33.52,33.74,33.9,34.36,34.5]},{"s":1,"a":35.29,"b":39.31,"text":"Say, work through this step by step, then give the final answer.","w":[35.42,36.08,36.36,36.58,36.74,37.02,37.22,38.02,38.28,38.5,38.62,38.96]},{"s":1,"a":39.61,"b":42.87,"text":"It often improves accuracy and lets you check the working.","w":[39.7,39.9,40.18,40.7,41.58,41.72,41.9,42.08,42.34,42.46]},{"s":2,"a":43.72,"b":49.95,"text":"Third, break a big task into smaller ones and do them in order. This is called prompt chaining.","w":[43.74,44.56,44.88,44.98,45.26,45.64,45.88,46.34,46.8,46.98,47.18,47.36,47.52,48.28,48.5,48.6,48.98,49.52]},{"s":2,"a":50.25,"b":58.33,"text":"First an outline, then one section at a time, then a review. One giant prompt usually gives a shallow answer.","w":[50.34,50.76,50.96,51.94,52.3,52.48,52.84,52.96,53.02,53.94,54.26,54.34,55.48,55.72,56.12,56.66,57.06,57.44,57.54,57.96]},{"s":2,"a":58.63,"b":68.18,"text":"Fourth, set the role, the audience and the format. Use clear separators, such as quotation marks or a line of dashes, around text you paste.","w":[58.66,59.42,59.64,59.76,60.42,60.64,61.18,61.28,61.4,62.46,62.7,63.02,64,64.26,64.42,65,65.62,65.78,65.84,66.08,66.18,66.98,67.3,67.6,67.76]},{"s":2,"a":68.48,"b":74.8,"text":"So the model knows what is instruction and what is material. The separators stop it confusing your notes with your request.","w":[68.54,68.72,68.84,69.18,69.52,69.7,69.84,70.74,70.9,71.12,71.24,72.06,72.2,72.74,73.04,73.14,73.6,73.78,74.02,74.18,74.32]},{"s":2,"a":75.1,"b":79.5,"text":"Then ask the model to check its own answer against your requirements, and refine.","w":[75.14,75.46,75.68,75.8,76.14,76.32,76.58,76.76,76.98,77.38,77.64,77.8,78.78,78.92]},{"s":3,"a":80.35,"b":83.75,"text":"Here is a worked example. You want flashcards from your notes.","w":[80.38,80.58,80.7,80.78,81.06,81.98,82.14,82.36,83,83.14,83.32]},{"s":3,"a":84.05,"b":92.07,"text":"Give two example cards in the exact format. Say the word Question, then the question, then the word Answer, then the answer.","w":[84.08,84.38,84.62,85.06,85.5,85.58,85.74,86.24,87.24,87.44,87.54,87.82,88.7,88.9,89.02,89.86,90.04,90.16,90.5,91.34,91.52,91.7]},{"s":3,"a":92.37,"b":97.57,"text":"Paste your notes between separators. The request is the instruction, and the notes are the material.","w":[92.38,92.66,92.86,93.16,93.6,94.64,94.78,95.3,95.42,95.56,96.5,96.6,96.74,97,97.12,97.22]},{"s":3,"a":97.87,"b":101.84,"text":"Ask for ten more cards in the same format, one fact per card.","w":[98.02,98.26,98.46,98.7,98.9,99.24,99.32,99.46,99.76,100.72,100.9,101.28,101.52]},{"s":4,"a":102.69,"b":104.98,"text":"You want an essay plan, then the essay.","w":[102.72,102.88,103.08,103.24,103.56,104.22,104.42,104.6]},{"s":4,"a":105.28,"b":109.25,"text":"Is it better to ask for everything in one prompt, or in steps, and why?","w":[105.34,105.48,105.64,105.96,106.22,106.44,106.66,107.06,107.26,107.44,107.98,108.12,108.24,108.74,108.88]},{"s":4,"a":109.7,"b":111.94,"text":"Pause here and try it. I'll wait.","w":[109.7,110.04,110.26,110.38,110.64,111.38,111.56]},{"s":5,"a":112.79,"b":118.95,"text":"In steps. Plan first, fix the plan, then write each part, so you can correct the direction early.","w":[112.84,113,113.82,114.2,114.94,115.22,115.32,116.06,116.3,116.66,116.86,117.34,117.5,117.62,117.78,118.12,118.22,118.64]},{"s":5,"a":119.25,"b":124.34,"text":"One giant prompt hides the problems until the very end, when it is too late to change course.","w":[119.44,119.66,120.06,120.42,120.74,120.86,121.3,121.56,121.66,122,122.6,122.78,122.9,123.04,123.26,123.5,123.62,123.9]},{"s":6,"a":125.19,"b":130.07,"text":"Examples that are all alike make the model copy them too closely. So vary them.","w":[125.28,125.82,125.98,126.18,126.4,127.04,127.24,127.36,127.7,128.02,128.22,128.46,129.34,129.54,129.86]},{"s":6,"a":130.37,"b":136.49,"text":"Asking for the answer first means the reasoning comes too late to improve it. Always ask for the reasoning first.","w":[130.52,130.9,131.02,131.18,131.52,131.82,132.08,132.24,132.7,133.04,133.34,133.56,133.72,134.1,134.78,135.2,135.42,135.56,135.68,136.08]},{"s":6,"a":136.79,"b":141.57,"text":"Changing five things at once means you cannot tell what helped. Fix one thing at a time.","w":[136.86,137.34,137.64,137.94,138.14,138.36,138.68,138.84,139.2,139.44,139.62,140.34,140.72,140.88,141.08,141.16,141.24]},{"s":6,"a":141.87,"b":147.01,"text":"And if you forget to state the format, you will get a shape you did not want. Say it clearly.","w":[141.92,142.06,142.14,142.3,142.58,142.74,143,143.14,144.12,144.24,144.4,144.56,144.64,144.9,145.06,145.22,145.42,146.22,146.44,146.58]},{"s":7,"a":147.86,"b":156.9,"text":"Recap. Give good varied examples. Ask for step by step reasoning. Chain big tasks in order, and set role, audience and format.","w":[147.94,148.82,149.06,149.32,149.66,150.76,151.04,151.24,151.48,151.64,151.9,152.78,153.14,153.38,154,154.18,154.76,154.94,155.2,155.7,156.2,156.36]},{"s":7,"a":157.2,"b":162.86,"text":"Check the answer against your requirements, then refine. Good prompts are clear, ordered and easy to check.","w":[157.28,157.52,157.7,158.02,158.28,158.44,159.36,159.6,160.46,160.7,161.04,161.18,161.68,162.06,162.26,162.52,162.62]},{"s":7,"a":163.16,"b":167.51,"text":"Top tip: keep one change per try, so you learn what works.","w":[163.2,163.52,164.18,164.56,164.76,165.12,165.3,166.2,166.36,166.56,166.84,167.04]},{"s":7,"a":167.81,"b":171.02,"text":"Knowzone is here to help you practise, one prompt at a time.","w":[167.88,168.38,168.5,168.7,168.82,169,169.14,170.02,170.2,170.54,170.62,170.7]}],"scenes":[{"at":0.25,"heading":"AI prompting","items":[{"at":0.4,"kind":"text","text":"Vague answer? It's often how you ask."},{"at":5.24,"kind":"text","text":"The way you ask matters."},{"at":9.14,"kind":"text","text":"4 prompt techniques that work"}]},{"at":13.96,"heading":"Technique 1: examples","items":[{"at":14.11,"kind":"text","text":"Give 1-3 input→output examples."},{"at":19.76,"kind":"term","text":"Few-shot prompting","detail":"Examples in prompt; zero shot = no examples."},{"at":24.32,"kind":"text","text":"Make examples good and varied."}]},{"at":28.37,"heading":"Technique 2: step by step","items":[{"at":28.52,"kind":"text","text":"Use for multi-step maths and logic."},{"at":35.19,"kind":"formula","text":"Step by step → final answer"},{"at":39.51,"kind":"text","text":"Improves accuracy; shows working."}]},{"at":43.47,"heading":"Techniques 3 & 4","items":[{"at":43.62,"kind":"term","text":"Prompt chaining","detail":"Break a big task into smaller tasks, in order."},{"at":50.15,"kind":"text","text":"Outline → sections → review; giant prompt = shallow."},{"at":58.53,"kind":"term","text":"Role, audience, format","detail":"Set all three; use clear separators around pasted text."},{"at":68.38,"kind":"text","text":"Separators mark instruction vs material."},{"at":75,"kind":"text","text":"Ask model to check against requirements, refine."}]},{"at":80.1,"heading":"Worked example: flashcards","items":[{"at":80.25,"kind":"text","text":"Goal: flashcards from your notes."},{"at":83.95,"kind":"text","text":"2 example cards: Question: … Answer: …"},{"at":92.27,"kind":"text","text":"Paste notes between separators."},{"at":97.77,"kind":"text","text":"Ask for 10 more cards, same format, 1 fact/card."}]},{"at":102.27,"heading":"Quick check","items":[{"at":102.27,"kind":"question","text":"You want an essay plan, then the essay. Is it better to ask for everything in one prompt, or in steps, and why?"}]},{"at":112.54,"heading":"Answer","items":[{"at":112.69,"kind":"text","text":"In steps: plan → fix plan → write parts."},{"at":119.15,"kind":"text","text":"One giant prompt hides problems until too late."}]},{"at":124.94,"heading":"Common mistakes","items":[{"at":125.09,"kind":"wrong","text":"Examples all alike"},{"at":130.27,"kind":"wrong","text":"Answer first, reasoning after"}]},{"at":136.54,"heading":"Common mistakes","items":[{"at":136.69,"kind":"wrong","text":"Change five things at once"},{"at":141.77,"kind":"wrong","text":"Forget to state format"}]},{"at":147.61,"heading":"Recap","items":[{"at":147.76,"kind":"text","text":"Good varied examples; step-by-step reasoning."},{"at":157.1,"kind":"text","text":"Chain tasks; set role, audience, format; check & refine."},{"at":163.06,"kind":"tip","text":"Keep one change per try, so you learn what works."},{"at":167.71,"kind":"text","text":"Knowzone: practise one prompt at a time."}]}],"check":{"at":112.19,"seconds":10,"question":"You want an essay plan, then the essay. Is it better to ask for everything in one prompt, or in steps, and why?","answer":"In steps: plan first, fix the plan, then write each part, so you can correct direction early."},"fps":30,"mouth":"","level":"","credits":[],"voice":"qwen3-tts-12hz-1.7b (cloned voice)"}